WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.140
Here's something nobody
prints on the label.

2
00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:04.981
Encryption has a shelf life.

3
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:11.046
Connections recorded today can be opened
later, once a quantum computer is big

4
00:00:11.086 --> 00:00:12.586
enough.

5
00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:18.895
You know your services need
quantum-safe encryption.

6
00:00:18.935 --> 00:00:21.141
The plan looks great on a slide.

7
00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:28.663
Then reality. The website,
the APIs, the database:

8
00:00:28.703 --> 00:00:31.489
they can't be rewritten in time.

9
00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:38.767
And that one system nobody dares
to touch? Nobody's touching it.

10
00:00:41.917 --> 00:00:47.194
Meet Acxelin QGuard. A product you
install and run yourself, not a service.

11
00:00:47.234 --> 00:00:52.795
It sits in front of the services you
already have, and makes the connections to

12
00:00:52.835 --> 00:00:56.877
them quantum-safe. The services
stay exactly as they are.

13
00:01:00.017 --> 00:01:02.706
Open the console and protect a service.

14
00:01:02.746 --> 00:01:06.454
Say what it is, its address,
and where it runs today.

15
00:01:09.617 --> 00:01:13.537
QGuard issues its certificate, writes its
own settings, and starts in front of the

16
00:01:13.577 --> 00:01:15.077
app.

17
00:01:23.517 --> 00:01:26.900
Not sure who's still on old
software? Start in watch mode.

18
00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:31.623
Everyone is served as usual, and QGuard
tells you how many would be refused, and

19
00:01:31.663 --> 00:01:33.163
why.

20
00:01:36.317 --> 00:01:39.538
When that list is empty,
turn on enforcement.

21
00:01:39.578 --> 00:01:43.636
Old clients are turned away.
Quantum-safe ones carry on.

22
00:01:46.783 --> 00:01:49.837
Every door needs a list
of who's allowed in.

23
00:01:49.877 --> 00:01:54.235
That's QCertificate: your own
certificate authority, built in.

24
00:01:56.783 --> 00:02:01.675
Issue a certificate for a partner, and
only that partner gets into your API.

25
00:02:04.817 --> 00:02:09.479
Partnership over? Revoke it, and
QGuard turns them away within a minute.

26
00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:20.567
Want to know how a
connection really behaves?

27
00:02:20.607 --> 00:02:23.731
Type an address, and get
the answer in plain words.

28
00:02:26.883 --> 00:02:30.971
It runs on your servers, and
nothing is sent to Acxelin Quantum.

29
00:02:31.011 --> 00:02:35.655
Key exchange and certificates both
follow NIST's post-quantum standards.

30
00:02:41.583 --> 00:02:43.043
One honest limit:

31
00:02:43.083 --> 00:02:48.951
browsers can't yet check quantum-safe
certificates, so public sites keep a regular

32
00:02:48.991 --> 00:02:51.945
one. The key exchange stays quantum-safe.

33
00:02:52.245 --> 00:02:55.998
Acxelin QGuard. Protect
what you can't rewrite.

